
X1. Turn off visualization during search runs.
In ngeneral.py, `visualize_search=True` stores every active cell and later renders plots. For `n=6, depth=15`, this is very expensive in both memory and time.
Use:
`visualize_search=False, visualize_mesh=False`

X2. Turn off or throttle progress/printing.
In search.py, progress and `new` prints happen in the hot loop. Console I/O is slow.
For fastest runs:
`show_progress=False`
If needed:
`progress_update_every=10000` (or higher).

x3. Stop doing full lower-bound recomputation for every child.
In bound.py, `energy_lower_bound(cell)` recomputes all pair terms from scratch, which is \(O(n^2)\) per node.
Carry parent bound forward and update only terms involving the split particle index. That reduces bound-update work to \(O(n)\) per child.

x4. Cache per-box corner points (or trigonometric terms).
In bound.py, bound.py, and energy.py, the same spherical conversions are repeated a lot.
Memoize `corner_points` and optionally midpoint cartesian points keyed by `(lo, hi)` tuples. This usually gives a noticeable speedup.

X5. Replace repeated object-heavy allocations in split.
In partition.py, each split deep-copies many dataclass objects.
Use lighter structures for hot paths (tuples/arrays), or a custom clone that only copies the one modified interval.

6. Improve branching strategy.
In partition.py, you split only the largest interval. Better pruning often comes from splitting the interval that most reduces uncertainty in energy bound, not just angular width.

7. Add an early-stop criterion when bound gap is small.
In search.py, you prune only via `lb >= best`.
Also stop globally when `best - current_global_lb < eps`. This can cut huge tail work.

x8. Parallelize child bound evaluation.
In search.py, each child’s `energy_lower_bound(child)` is independent.
Use multiprocessing for bound calculations (process pool), especially for larger `n`.

x9. Use Numba/Cython for numerical kernels.
The hot loops are in bound.py and energy.py.
JIT-compiling those loops can provide large speedups with minimal algorithm changes.

x10. Keep symmetry reduction strict in initialization.
inital_part.py already fixes some coordinates. Tightening this further (without excluding valid minima) reduces search volume substantially.

Most immediate wins for you:
1) disable visualizations during compute,
2) reduce printing/progress frequency,
3) incremental lower-bound updates from parent to child.  
Those three alone should make a major difference.